  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of what the retrieval traced:\n\n**Root cause chain:**\n1. `pkg/registry/core/rest/storage_core.go` registers the PostStartHook `\"start-service-ip-repair-controllers\"`, which waits up to 1 minute for both the ClusterIP and NodePort repair loops to signal `onFirstSuccess`, else returns `\"unable to perform initial IP and Port allocation check\"`.\n2. `pkg/registry/core/service/ipallocator/controller/repairip.go` (`RepairIPAddress.RunUntil`) runs the ClusterIP-side repair loop. Its initial `runOnce()` wraps `doRunOnce()` in `retry.RetryOnConflict` \u2014 which **only retries on HTTP 409 Conflict**. `doRunOnce()`/`syncService()` calls `client.NetworkingV1beta1().IPAddresses().Create(...)` for any Service missing its IPAddress object. If that create returns any other error type, `RunUntil` logs it and **returns immediately without ever calling `onFirstSuccess`** \u2014 permanently starving the PostStartHook's WaitGroup.\n3. The `Create` on a freshly-started (upgraded) apiserver hits `MutatingAdmissionWebhook`/`ValidatingAdmissionWebhook` (`staging/.../admission/plugin/webhook/generic/webhook.go`, same pattern in `.../policy/generic/plugin.go` for ValidatingAdmissionPolicy) \u2014 both default-on per `pkg/kubeapiserver/options/plugins.go`. Their `Dispatch()` calls `a.WaitForReady()`; if the plugin's own informers (namespace/webhook-config/policy source) haven't synced within the 10s window (`timeToWaitForReady` in `staging/.../admission/handler.go`), it returns `admission.NewForbidden(attr, fmt.Errorf(\"not yet ready to handle request\"))` \u2014 exactly the `ipaddresses.networking.k8s.io \"...\" is forbidden: not yet ready to handle request` seen in the logs.\n4. That Forbidden (403) is not a Conflict, so it isn't retried, kills the repair loop's first pass, the PostStartHook times out after 1 minute, and readyz then reports `informer-sync,poststarthook/start-service-ip-repair-controllers check failed`.\n\nThis is a genuine race: the repair controller starts creating IPAddress objects essentially as soon as its informers/ServiceCIDR sync, but that can be *before* the apiserver's own admission-webhook/policy plumbing finishes its independent (10s) readiness warm-up \u2014 and the repair controller's error handling treats any non-Conflict failure as fatal for that startup attempt rather than retrying, so it never gets a second chance within the 1-minute PostStartHook budget.",
